{
    "title": "International Registered Mail",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "November 25, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-25/pdf/2025-21089.pdf",
    "summary": "Big changes are coming to international mail and to how certain communications devices get approved for the U.S. market. The Postal Service is tightening when you can use Registered Mail for items going overseas, and the Federal Communications Commission is closing loopholes that let high\u2011risk hardware slip through approvals.\n\nFor international mail, starting January 1, 2026, Registered Mail will be available only for letter mail that contains documents. It will no longer be offered as an extra service for packages sent abroad, including items mailed as free matter for the blind. The Postal Service is also retiring references to the International Surface Air Lift program and making housekeeping edits throughout its manual to reflect current services. Country\u2011specific rules are being updated too. Indonesia\u2019s postal operator asks mailers to include the recipient\u2019s mobile number and email on labels and customs forms to speed up customs and delivery. Shipments to Libya remain tightly limited to donations of food, clothing, medicines, and medical supplies intended strictly for medical purposes, and those limits apply to small packages and priority airmail items.\n\nWho benefits from the postal changes? Senders of documents still have access to Registered Mail, and clearer standards should reduce confusion for mailers and postal staff. Indonesian recipients could see faster customs clearance, and the Libya rule continues to prioritize humanitarian relief. Who may be negatively affected? Small businesses and individuals who relied on Registered Mail for added security on international packages will need to choose other options. Some shippers may be uncomfortable adding a recipient\u2019s phone or email on labels. Mail consolidators that used the retired surface air lift program will have to adjust workflows or pricing.\n\nOn the communications side, the Federal Communications Commission confirmed that its ban on approving \u201ccovered\u201d high\u2011risk equipment applies not only to complete devices but also to plug\u2011in radio modules, and to any device that contains those modules. The agency also created a process to curb previously granted approvals so that covered products can no longer be imported or marketed, and it clarified how to interpret who \u201cproduced\u201d a covered item and what counts as an impermissible modification.\n\nThese steps aim to strengthen national security and give manufacturers clearer rules of the road. At the same time, some device makers and importers may face redesign costs, fewer sourcing options, and potential delays bringing products to market, which could ripple to retailers and consumers through higher prices or reduced choice.",
